4. How JavaScript Works in the Browser

When you open a website, the browser loads:

HTML (structure)
CSS (design)
JavaScript (functionality)

The browser has a JavaScript engine:

Chrome uses V8 Engine
Firefox uses SpiderMonkey

The JavaScript engine:

Reads the code
Converts it into machine code
Executes it

Main components involved:

Call Stack
Executes functions one by one
Web APIs
Provided by the browser
Examples: setTimeout, DOM events
Callback Queue
Stores completed asynchronous tasks
Event Loop
Moves tasks from the queue to the call stack

Simple concept:

Synchronous code runs first
Asynchronous code runs later

Quick Summary
JavaScript is used to make web pages interactive
Created in 1995 by Brendan Eich
Used in frontend, backend, apps, and testing
Works using JavaScript engine, call stack, and event loop
